            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"DIVERGENT\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"Egypt is overwhelmingly understood as a symbol of bondage to sin, the flesh, and the secular world-system opposed to God, based on the Exodus typology of deliverance.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"Egypt symbolizes apostate Jerusalem (the whore of Babylon), the specific city that kills the prophets and where the Lord was crucified.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"The world-system and bondage to sin.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"Apostate Jerusalem / the whore of Babylon.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"CONTRADICTS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"DIFFERENT_REFERENT\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The consensus applies Egypt universally to the unredeemed world and spiritual bondage. The book restricts its spiritual meaning to a specific historical/covenantal entity: Jerusalem.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"The consensus views Egypt as the pagan world from which God's people are delivered; the book identifies Egypt as the corrupted covenant city (Jerusalem) itself, directly rejecting the generic 'world-system' interpretation.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book elevates a specific minority interpretation of Revelation 11:8 (Egypt as Jerusalem) to the primary spiritual meaning, explicitly contrasting it with the commonly taught view of Egypt as the 'world-system'.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"While Revelation 11:8 explicitly links 'Egypt' to the city where the Lord was crucified, the vast majority of biblical references to Egypt (especially in the foundational Exodus narrative) refer to a pagan, external empire from which Israel is saved, heavily supporting the broader typological reading of Egypt as the unredeemed world or bondage to sin.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
